INT. LAMPLIGHTERS' GUILD - COPPER ROOM - NIGHT

1887. Gas London. A narrow workshop lined with lamp-heads, brass keys, and tin oil cans. A single jet burns low over a scarred table.

CORNELIUS AGNEW, 70, stooped, spectacles on a cord, polishes a lamp-glass with a rag. He does it too slowly. He has all night.

The door opens. Cold fog drifts in.

TOBIAS HALE, 28, wet through, bowler in hand, ink-stained cuffs, enters. He looks around like a man who has been here before.

                    TOBIAS
Mr. Agnew.

                    CORNELIUS
You're late.

                    TOBIAS
The omnibus broke an axle.

                    CORNELIUS
I said ten o'clock.

                    TOBIAS
It is ten o'clock.

Cornelius lifts the glass to the jet. Squints through it.

                    CORNELIUS
Quarter past, by my reckoning.

Tobias lets that sit. Hangs his hat on a hook. Doesn't sit.

                    TOBIAS
You asked me here.

                    CORNELIUS
I did.

                    TOBIAS
You said it was about the Pettigrew matter.

                    CORNELIUS
It is.

He sets the glass down. Lifts another. Starts again.

A long beat. A long, patient beat.

                    TOBIAS
Mr. Agnew, I have an editor waiting on copy.

                    CORNELIUS
Let him wait.

                    TOBIAS
I can't.

                    CORNELIUS
You can. It's an extraordinary thing, waiting. You'd be surprised what a man will say when you let him.

                    TOBIAS
Is that what this is?

                    CORNELIUS
I'm an old man. I'm only polishing.

Tobias crosses to the table. Looks at the lamp-glass. At the old man's hands, trembling slightly.

                    TOBIAS
Eleven lamps went dark on Marlow Street the night Pettigrew died.

                    CORNELIUS
Eleven.

                    TOBIAS
Someone put them out.

                    CORNELIUS
The wind.

                    TOBIAS
There was no wind.

Cornelius puts down the rag. Takes off his spectacles. Folds them.

                    CORNELIUS
You've done your homework.

                    TOBIAS
I've done nothing yet.

                    CORNELIUS
Then you'll want to sit.

Tobias stays standing. Cornelius shrugs and lowers himself onto a stool, bones cracking.

                    CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
I was told to do it. By a man in a good coat. He paid me forty sovereigns.

                    TOBIAS
Who?

                    CORNELIUS
I don't know his name.

                    TOBIAS
Would you know his face?

                    CORNELIUS
I'd know his coat.

Tobias takes out a notebook. Writes. The pencil scratches loud in the quiet.

                    TOBIAS
Why are you telling me?

                    CORNELIUS
Because I've lit those lamps for fifty-one years. And I put out eleven in one night so a man could be killed in the dark.

He looks up. His eyes are wet and clear.

                    CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
That's a hard thing to polish off.

Tobias stops writing. Looks at him a while.

                    TOBIAS
They'll hang you.

                    CORNELIUS
Probably.

                    TOBIAS
I should say I won't print it.

                    CORNELIUS
But you will.

                    TOBIAS
I will.

                    CORNELIUS
Good lad.

Somewhere in the fog outside, a church bell tolls the half hour.

                    TOBIAS
(quiet)
I'm sorry.

                    CORNELIUS
(quiet)
Don't be.

They speak at the same time.

                    TOBIAS
You could run.

                    CORNELIUS
^
I'd like to finish my round.

They both stop. The jet hisses. Tobias lowers the pencil.

                    TOBIAS
Your round.

                    CORNELIUS
Marlow Street. Eleven lamps. I put them out. I'd like to put them back.

Tobias puts the notebook in his pocket. Takes his hat from the hook.

                    TOBIAS
I'll walk with you.

                    CORNELIUS
You'll get wet.

                    TOBIAS
I'm already wet.

Cornelius almost smiles. Rises with effort. Collects a long brass pole from the corner, a hooked key hanging from its tip.

He blows out the jet on the way past. They walk out together into the fog.

EXT. MARLOW STREET - NIGHT

Dark. Eleven dead lamps in a long row.

Cornelius lifts the pole to the first one. A click. A flare. A pool of gold on wet cobble.

He moves to the next. Tobias walks a step behind, hat in his hands, saying nothing.

One by one, the street comes back to life.
